Here are both approaches using Shell navigation and `IQueryAttributable`:

**1. Register the route**
```csharp
// AppShell.xaml.cs
Routing.RegisterRoute("productdetails", typeof(ProductDetailsPage));
```

**2. Navigate from ProductListPage**
```csharp
// Pass ID as a string query parameter
await Shell.Current.GoToAsync($"productdetails?productId={product.Id}");

// Pass the full object via a dictionary
await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("productdetails", new Dictionary<string, object>
{
    { "product", selectedProduct }
});
```

**3. Receive data in the ViewModel**
```csharp
public partial class ProductDetailsViewModel : ObservableObject, IQueryAttributable
{
    [ObservableProperty] string productId;
    [ObservableProperty] Product product;

    public void ApplyQueryAttributes(IDictionary<string, object> query)
    {
        // String parameter — value arrives as a string
        if (query.TryGetValue("productId", out var id))
            ProductId = id.ToString();

        // Complex object — value arrives as the original object
        if (query.TryGetValue("product", out var obj) && obj is Product p)
            Product = p;
    }
}
```

**4. Wire the ViewModel to the page**
```csharp
public partial class ProductDetailsPage : ContentPage
{
    public ProductDetailsPage(ProductDetailsViewModel vm)
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        BindingContext = vm;
    }
}
```

**Key differences:**

| | String query param | Dictionary object |
|---|---|---|
| **Syntax** | `$"route?key={val}"` | `GoToAsync("route", dict)` |
| **Type received** | `string` | Original CLR type |
| **URI-safe** | Yes | N/A (not in URI) |
| **Use when** | Simple IDs, flags | Complex/nested objects |

Shell calls `ApplyQueryAttributes` on both the page and its `BindingContext` if either implements `IQueryAttributable`, so putting it on the ViewModel keeps your page code-behind clean.
